/**
 * Route templates, and the one function that turns a pathname into a span
 * name — `routePatternForSpan`.
 *
 * It lives beside `trace-privacy.ts` because both the server and the browser
 * name their spans with it, and core is the package both can import.
 *
 * MOST OF THIS FILE IS GENERATED. The block between the BEGIN and END markers
 * is written by `bun run routes:table` from the server's route table
 * (`packages/server/src/route-table-rows.ts`), one template per
 * fixed-length row. Add a route by adding its row and running that command;
 * never edit the block by hand. `packages/server/test/route-table.test.ts`
 * fails when the block is not what the rows render to, and
 * `packages/server/test/span-route-names.test.ts` fails on any row this file
 * cannot name.
 *
 * Why naming matters. A route missing from the list costs no privacy (see
 * `ROUTE_TEMPLATES`), but it costs the name: every segment collapses to `:id`,
 * so unrelated routes arrive at Sentry under one description. 106 of 271 rows
 * were missing when the list was first checked against the table, which is
 * why a single browser N+1 group covered three different pages — on the doc
 * page it was `…/docs/<id>/meeting-bot` and `…/docs/<id>/notes-method`, two
 * different calls reading as `GET /:id/:id/:id/:id/:id` twice.
 */

